@subsection Test conventions

@itemize
@item
@strong{No live network.}  All LLM calls are mocked with
@code{patch("codebud.planner.call_llm", ...)} or equivalent.  CLI tests replace the agent with a fake through
@code{codebud.cli.common.make_agent}, the one place every subcommand
creates it.

@item
@strong{Local by default, proven.}  @file{test_local_only.py} refuses
every connection to a non-loopback address and runs @command{codebud run},
@command{codebud index} and a search against a fake Ollama on
@code{127.0.0.1}, with @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} set; any new code path that
reached outward would fail it.

@item
@strong{No live Ollama required.}  @code{test_llm_stub.py} patches
@code{requests.get} and @code{requests.post} to simulate server responses.

@item
@strong{Fixtures are local.}  Helper factories (@code{_make_registry()},
@code{_make_safety()}, @code{_good_plan()}) are defined at the top of each
test file.  The shared @file{conftest.py} only gives every test its own
data and config directories and turns on the offline embedding stub.

@item
@strong{Optional dependencies skip, never fail.}  Tests that need
@code{numpy} (the @code{rag} extra) are marked @code{needs_numpy} and
skip without it, so @command{make check} passes with a Python that has
only the core dependencies.

@item
@strong{Class-per-concern.}  Tests are grouped into classes
(@code{TestPromptContent}, @code{TestRetryLogic}, @code{TestValidation})
for readability without requiring the overhead of @code{unittest.TestCase}.
@end itemize


@node Code Style
@section Code Style
@cindex ruff
@cindex code style
@cindex PEP 8

Code style is enforced by @command{ruff}.  The configuration lives in
@file{pyproject.toml} under @code{[tool.ruff.lint]}:

@example
make lint      @r{# check for style violations}
make format    @r{# auto-format + auto-fix (safe fixes only)}
@end example

@strong{Active rule sets:}

@table @code
@item E, W
@command{pycodestyle} errors and warnings.

@item F
@command{pyflakes} (unused imports, undefined names).

@item I
@command{isort} (import ordering: stdlib → third-party → first-party).

@item UP
@command{pyupgrade} (replace deprecated @code{typing.Dict}/List/Tuple etc.@:
with built-in equivalents for Python @geq{}3.10).

@item B
@command{flake8-bugbear} (@code{zip(strict=True)}, ambiguous variable
names, etc.).
@end table

Line length is 100 characters.  @code{E501} (line-too-long) is suppressed
because @command{ruff format} handles wrapping.

Type annotations use built-in generics (@code{dict}, @code{list},
@code{tuple}) rather than @code{typing.Dict} etc., and the
@code{X | None} union syntax instead of @code{Optional[X]}.  Both are
available from Python 3.10 onward, which is the minimum supported version.


@node Adding a Tool
@section Adding a Tool
@cindex adding a tool

Follow this five-step checklist to add a new tool.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Create the tool class.}  Add a new file (or a class to an existing
file) under @file{src/codebud/tools/}.  The class must have:

@example
class MyTool:
    description = "One sentence for the planner prompt."
    usage_hint  = (
        "When to use me and what args are required. "
        'Required args: "foo" (str), "bar" (int).'
    )

    def run(self, args: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:
        foo = args.get("foo")
        if not foo:
            return @{"stdout": "", "stderr": "Missing: foo", "returncode": 1@}
        # ... do work ...
        return @{"stdout": result, "stderr": "", "returncode": 0@}
@end example

@item
@strong{Register it.}  In @file{src/codebud/tools/tool_registry.py},
add an import and a @code{_register} call:

@example
from .my_module import MyTool
# ... inside __init__ ...
self._register("my_tool", MyTool())
@end example

@item
@strong{Add to the safety policy} (if the tool runs shell commands).
Add the commands the tool produces to @file{harmless_commands.json} or
@file{powerful_commands.json} (@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).

@item
@strong{Write tests.}  Add a test class to the appropriate file in
@file{tests/}.  At minimum:

@itemize
@item
Test that the tool returns @code{returncode=0} and the expected
@code{stdout} on valid input.

@item
Test that it returns @code{returncode=1} when required args are missing.
@end itemize

@item
@strong{Add a few-shot example} to @code{LLMPlanner._FEW_SHOT_EXAMPLES}
in @file{src/codebud/planner.py} if the new tool represents a common pattern.
Without an example, the planner will rarely use it.
@end enumerate

@subsection Checklist summary

@example
[ ] src/codebud/tools/my_module.py      — class with description, usage_hint, run()
[ ] src/codebud/tools/tool_registry.py  — registered with _register()
[ ] ~/.config/codebud/*_commands.json   — policy updated if needed
[ ] tests/                              — new test cases pass
[ ] src/codebud/planner.py              — few-shot example added
@end example


@node Tuning the Planner Prompt
@section Tuning the Planner Prompt
@cindex planner prompt, tuning
@cindex few-shot examples

The planner prompt text is in @code{LLMPlanner._render_prompt()} in
@file{src/codebud/planner.py}; @code{_build_prompt()} calls it and trims
the conversation history to fit the context window.  The most effective ways to improve JSON
reliability are:

@table @strong
@item Add or refine few-shot examples
The class attribute @code{_FEW_SHOT_EXAMPLES} contains five worked
examples.  If the model consistently picks the wrong tool for a specific
pattern, add an example that shows the correct choice.  The format is:

@example
Example N:
User: "<user message>"
@{"status": "ok", "plan": [...]@}
@end example

Every planner prompt starts with the same text, which lets Ollama reuse its
cache between requests; changing the examples makes the next request of
each session a little slower once.

@item Add a CRITICAL RULE
The prompt has a numbered @samp{CRITICAL RULES} section at the top.
Add a rule when the model makes a systematic mistake.  For example, rule 3
was added because the 3B model repeatedly used @code{file_read} with
@code{path: "."} instead of @code{command} with @code{cmd: "ls"}.

@item Improve usage_hint text
Each tool class has a @code{usage_hint} attribute.  @code{ToolRegistry.describe_tools()}
includes it in the prompt under the tool's name.  Make the hint concrete:
say what the required args are and give a one-line @code{@{"arg": "value"@}}
example.

@item Test with a fixed battery
After any prompt change, run a fixed set of requests and compare the
plans, for example:

@example
codebud plan "list files in current directory"
codebud plan "read README.md and summarize it"
codebud plan "run the tests and tell me which ones fail"
codebud plan "create hello.py that prints Hello, world"
codebud plan "refactor src/codebud/planner.py to add a docstring at the top"
@end example

The @option{--json} flag lets you inspect the raw plan for each.  Track
how many plans are correct to measure whether the change helped.
@end table


@node Adding a Safety Rule
@section Adding a Safety Rule
@cindex safety rules, adding

@subsection Allow a new command

Add the command to @file{harmless_commands.json} (your copy in
@file{~/.config/codebud/}, or @file{src/codebud/data/policy/} in the source tree to change the
shipped default):

@example
[
  "echo", "cat", "head", "tail", "ls", "pwd", "whoami", "wc",
  "find", "diff", "git log", "git status", "git diff", "git show",
  "tree"
]
@end example

Harmless commands only read or report.  A command that runs code or
changes files (an interpreter, a test runner, a build tool) belongs in
@file{powerful_commands.json} instead, so it is confirmed before running
(@pxref{Powerful Commands}).

Save the file.  The change takes effect on the next @command{codebud run}
invocation without restarting anything.

@subsection Restrict a command

Remove the entry from the JSON file.  The @code{SafetyEngine} will reject
any plan step whose command's words begin with that entry.

@subsection Add a catastrophic-command refusal

Refusals that hold whatever the policy says cannot be added via config
files; they live in @code{catastrophic_reason()} in
@file{src/codebud/safety.py}, which judges the parsed command (wrappers
such as @command{sudo} already removed by @code{_unwrap()}).  Add a
program name to @code{_POWER}, a directory to @code{_VITAL}, or a new
check on @code{name} and @code{args}, and a case to
@file{tests/test_catastrophic.py}; the planner and the sandbox both use
the function.


@node Project Conventions
@section Project Conventions
@cindex conventions
@cindex naming conventions

@table @strong
@item Virtual environment
Always @file{.venv/} (not @file{venv/}).  Created by @command{./bootstrap --dev}
or @command{make install-dev}.

@item Editable install
Use @command{make install-dev} (which runs @code{pip install -e . --group dev}).
This places the @command{codebud} binary in @file{.venv/bin/codebud} and
makes the @code{codebud} package (in @file{src/codebud/}) importable
without @code{PYTHONPATH} hacks.

@item No comments by default
Comments are reserved for non-obvious constraints or workarounds.  Code
that reads naturally from its identifiers should have no comment.

@item No @code{typing.Dict}/List/Optional
Use built-in generics: @code{dict[str, Any]}, @code{list[str]},
@code{X | None}.  These are available in Python 3.10+.

@item Tool contract
Every tool @code{run()} method returns exactly:
@code{@{"stdout": str, "stderr": str, "returncode": int@}}.  Never raise
an exception from @code{run()} --- catch and return @code{returncode=1}.

@item Commit style
GNU-style: short subject line, blank line, body paragraphs.  One commit
per tested step.  No @code{Co-Authored-By} trailer.

@item Tests first
Write at least one test before adding a new feature.  All tests must pass
without a live Ollama server.

@item Continuous integration
GitHub Actions (@file{.github/workflows/ci.yml}) runs @command{make check}
on Ubuntu and macOS (oldest and newest supported Python) and on Fedora for
every push and pull request.  The Codeberg repository is a mirror and runs
no CI of its own.
@end table


@node Starting from a Clean Checkout
@section Starting from a Clean Checkout
@cindex clean checkout
@cindex maintainer-clean

The steps below take a working copy back to its sources, rebuild it, run
the tests and start Codebud.  None of them touch your Codebud data
(@file{~/.local/share/codebud}) or configuration
(@file{~/.config/codebud}).

@strong{Do not use @command{git clean -dfx} to reset the tree.}  It
deletes every file Git does not track, including directories that are
deliberately kept out of Git and exist nowhere else, such as a local
@file{notes/} directory or editor and agent settings (@file{.claude/}).
Clean with the build system instead.

@subheading 1. Clean the build products

@example
make maintainer-clean       @r{# configure, Makefiles, the built manual}
rm -rf autom4te.cache build-aux build src/codebud.egg-info \
       codebud-*.tar.gz configure~ .pytest_cache .ruff_cache
find src tests -name __pycache__ -type d -prune -exec rm -rf @{@} +
@end example

@noindent
@command{make maintainer-clean} needs a configured tree; if there is no
@file{Makefile}, skip it.  To rebuild the Python environment as well
(this needs the network):

@example
rm -rf .venv && python -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -e ".[rag,crypto]" --group dev
@end example

@subheading 2. Build and test

@example
./bootstrap
./configure PYTHON="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"
make
make check        @r{# the offline test suite; no Ollama needed}
make distcheck    @r{# optional: the full release check}
@end example

@noindent
The summary at the end of @command{configure} shows what was found
(@code{numpy}, @code{pyrage}, @command{bwrap}, @command{ollama}); the
tests that need a missing optional module are skipped, not failed.

@subheading 3. Start Ollama and run Codebud

@example
ollama serve > /tmp/ollama.log 2>&1 &   @r{# unless it runs as a service}
ollama list                             @r{# the models you have}
export PATH="$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH"
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M
codebud doctor
codebud chat
@end example

@noindent
Try it on another project rather than on Codebud's own checkout: there
Codebud warns, correctly, that edits can change the code its next run
executes (@pxref{What Is Not Protected}).


@node Versioning
@section Versioning
@cindex versioning
@cindex semantic versioning

Codebud follows @uref{https://semver.org, Semantic Versioning}: a version
is @var{major}.@var{minor}.@var{patch}.  The single source of the number
is @code{version} in @file{pyproject.toml}; @command{configure}, the
release tarball, the package metadata and @command{codebud version} all
read it from there.

@subheading While the version is 0.x

Codebud is not yet at 1.0, so its interface --- the command line, the
configuration keys and files, the stored data formats --- may still
change.  Until 1.0:

@itemize
@item @strong{minor} (0.1.0 @result{} 0.2.0): any new feature, and any
change that is not backwards compatible (a renamed option, a setting
that now means something else, a data format that older versions cannot
read);
@item @strong{patch} (0.1.0 @result{} 0.1.1): bug fixes, security fixes
and documentation only, with nothing the user has to change.
@end itemize

@subheading From 1.0 on

1.0.0 is the promise that the interface is stable.  After it:
@strong{major} for any incompatible change, @strong{minor} for a new
feature that keeps compatibility, @strong{patch} for fixes.

@subheading When the number changes

The version is changed when a release is cut, not with every commit:
each change lands under an ``unreleased'' heading in @file{NEWS}, and
the release bumps the version by the largest kind of change since the
last one (one incompatible change makes the next 0.x release a minor
one, however many fixes come with it).  A change that breaks
compatibility says so in its commit message (@samp{BREAKING}) and in
@file{NEWS}, with what the user has to do.

@subheading Checking a release
@cindex release check

@command{make release-check} says whether the checkout is ready to be
released.  It never tags, uploads or fetches anything, and it lists every
problem at once rather than stopping at the first:

@itemize
@item the checkout is on @code{main}, clean, and every remote's
@code{main} is at @code{HEAD} (as last fetched or pushed);
@item no tag @samp{v@var{version}} exists yet;
@item the newest @file{NEWS} entry is this version and carries its
release date instead of @samp{(unreleased)};
@item @command{ruff check} and @command{ruff format --check} pass;
@item @command{make distcheck} passes;
@item the sdist and wheel build, pass @command{twine check}, and the
wheel installs into a fresh virtual environment where
@command{codebud version} reports this version.  This step downloads the
runtime dependencies.
@end itemize

It needs the development tools (@command{ruff}, @code{build},
@command{twine}) in the configured Python, so configure with
@samp{PYTHON="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"} after @command{make install-dev}.
@samp{scripts/release-check --quick @var{srcdir} @var{python}} runs only
the fast checks (everything before @command{make distcheck}).

@subheading Publishing a release
@cindex publishing a release
@cindex PyPI, publishing to
@cindex trusted publishing

Releases reach PyPI through @dfn{trusted publishing}: the GitHub Actions
workflow @file{.github/workflows/release.yml} proves to PyPI who it is
with a short-lived OpenID Connect token, so no API token is stored
anywhere, and PyPI attaches signed attestations to every file.  Pushing
a tag @samp{v@var{version}} builds the sdist and wheel once, publishes
them to TestPyPI and, only if that succeeded, to PyPI.  The workflow
stops when the tag does not match @code{version} in
@file{pyproject.toml}.  Starting the workflow by hand (@samp{Run
workflow} on its Actions page, or @samp{gh workflow run release.yml})
publishes to TestPyPI only: a rehearsal.

Once, before the first release, the maintainer registers the workflow
on both sites (@samp{Publishing} @result{} @samp{Add a new pending
publisher} on @uref{https://pypi.org} and @uref{https://test.pypi.org},
each account with two-factor authentication):

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Field @tab Value
@item PyPI project name @tab @code{codebud}
@item Owner @tab @code{mkhellat}
@item Repository name @tab @code{codebud}
@item Workflow name @tab @code{release.yml}
@item Environment name @tab @code{pypi} (on PyPI), @code{testpypi} (on TestPyPI)
@end multitable

@noindent
The project name is claimed by its first upload.

Release tags and the GNU tarball are signed with an OpenPGP key kept for
Codebud alone.  Once, the maintainer creates it and tells Git to use it:

@example
gpg --quick-gen-key "Codebud release signing <@var{address}>" ed25519 sign 2y
gpg --list-secret-keys --keyid-format long       @r{# note the fingerprint}
git config user.signingkey @var{fingerprint}     @r{# in this checkout only}
gpg --armor --export @var{fingerprint} > codebud-signing-key.asc
@end example

@noindent
and publishes the public key (@file{codebud-signing-key.asc}) where users
can check it against the fingerprint: the key's settings on GitHub and
Codeberg, and @uref{https://keys.openpgp.org}.  The private key and its
passphrase never leave the maintainer's machine, and the expiry date is
extended (@samp{gpg --quick-set-expire}) before it runs out.  In the GitHub
repository settings, the @code{pypi} environment can be limited to
@samp{v*} tags and made to wait for the maintainer's approval.

To cut a release:

@enumerate
@item Decide the version from @file{NEWS} (the largest kind of change
since the last release, @pxref{Versioning}), set it in
@file{pyproject.toml} and in the newest @file{NEWS} heading, and replace
@samp{(unreleased)} there with today's date, e.g.
@samp{* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (2026-11-20) [alpha]};
commit and push.
@item Run @command{make release-check} until it passes.
@item Optionally rehearse on TestPyPI (run the workflow by hand), then
install from there into a fresh virtual environment:
@example
pip install --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ \
    --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple/ codebud
@end example
@item Sign the release tarball that @command{make release-check} built
(it ran @command{make distcheck}):
@example
make release-sign      @r{# codebud-0.1.0.tar.gz.sig and .sha256}
@end example
@item Tag with a signature and push the tag to both remotes:
@example
git tag -s v0.1.0 -m "codebud 0.1.0"
git tag -v v0.1.0
git push github v0.1.0
git push origin v0.1.0
@end example
@item Watch the @samp{Release} workflow; when it is done, the release is
on PyPI.
@item Make the GitHub release, with the @file{NEWS} entry as its notes
and the tarball, signature and checksum attached:
@example
gh release create v0.1.0 --title "codebud 0.1.0" --notes-file @var{notes} \
    codebud-0.1.0.tar.gz codebud-0.1.0.tar.gz.sig codebud-0.1.0.tar.gz.sha256
@end example
@noindent
and the same on Codeberg (@samp{Releases} @result{} @samp{New release},
for the existing tag).
@item Start the next version: add a heading
@samp{* Noteworthy changes in release @var{next} (unreleased)} on top of
@file{NEWS}, with @var{next} a provisional guess.  @file{pyproject.toml}
keeps the released version until the next release is cut; the test
suite accepts exactly this, an unreleased entry above the released
version's own.
@end enumerate

